<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1) Plug-and-play <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite></span>: Asking the LLM to generate an answer directly from symptoms is like a closed-book exam—the model relies only on its internal knowledge without external references.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2) FT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite></span>: Create a TCM formula instruction dataset and fine-tune the LLM to generate symptom-based answers. This resembles reviewing material before a closed-book exam.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3) RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite></span>: Require the LLM generate answers using symptoms and unstructured reference documents. This is like an open-book exam with unsorted reference materials.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4) GraphRAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite></span>: Ask the LLM to generates answers using symptoms and reference documents partitioned into <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> subtopics via community detection. This mirrors an open-book exam with systematically organized materials.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5) RAG+FT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite></span>: Fine-tune the LLM using a dataset where it generates answers from symptoms and unstructured reference documents. This process resembles a practice open-book exam before the actual one—both allow referencing mixed, unorganized materials.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6) GraphRAG+FT</span>: Fine-tune the LLM using an instruction dataset where it generates answers from symptoms and reference documents partitioned into <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> subtopics via community detection. This mimics a practice open-book exam with categorized materials before the actual exam.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Existing approaches (Solutions 1) - 5)) face limitations in addressing TCM formula tasks. Solutions 1) - 2) lack reference documents, relying solely on the model’s internal knowledge, which proves inadequate for domain-specific challenges. While Solutions 3) - 5) incorporate reference documents, they either lack fine-grained division (hindering precise retrieval) or omit LLM fine-tuning (limiting information integration). In contrast, Solution 6) combines GraphRAG’s summarization/generalization capabilities with fine-tuned LLMs, enabling precise adaptation to TCM formula tasks. Therefore, ZhiFangDanTai implements Solution 6), integrating GraphRAG with LLM fine-tuning for optimal performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GraphRAG</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Current TCM formula datasets provide descriptions but lack detailed explanations. To generate comprehensive explanations, it is necessary to augment these datasets with fine-grained information from external sources. As discussed in Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="II-C Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation ‣ II Related Work ‣ ZhiFangDanTai: Fine-tuning Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation Model for Traditional Chinese Medicine Formula" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-C</span></a>, both RAG and GraphRAG can retrieve useful knowledge for improving LLM-generated responses. However, GraphRAG outperforms RAG in summarizing and generalizing text-block content. To this end, we utilize GraphRAG to solve the TCM formula task.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we divide GraphRAG into retrieval and generation phases. In retrieval phrase (Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="III-B1 Collect Original Dataset→Create Source Documents ‣ III-B GraphRAG ‣ III ZhiFangDanTai Model ‣ ZhiFangDanTai: Fine-tuning Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation Model for Traditional Chinese Medicine Formula" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B1</span></a> - Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS5" title="III-B5 Knowledge Graph → Graph Communities → Community Summaries ‣ III-B GraphRAG ‣ III ZhiFangDanTai Model ‣ ZhiFangDanTai: Fine-tuning Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation Model for Traditional Chinese Medicine Formula" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B5</span></a>), we build a knowledge graph <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> from reference documents <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathit{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathit{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> and derive <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> community summaries by clustering related entities. In generation phrase (Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS6" title="III-B6 Community Summaries → Community Answers → Global Answer ‣ III-B GraphRAG ‣ III ZhiFangDanTai Model ‣ ZhiFangDanTai: Fine-tuning Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation Model for Traditional Chinese Medicine Formula" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B6</span></a>), given symptom descriptions <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we adopt a map-reduce approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>: the map phase retrieves answers from each community summary, while the reduce phase summarize these fine-grained results. The combined output—along with the original question—is then fed into the LLM to further perform SFT and DPO phrases. The details of GraphRAG are as follows:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>1 </span>Collect Original Dataset<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>Create Source Documents </h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We collect 80,000 pieces of TCM formula data from the Chinese Formula Database<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              https://www.ncmi.cn/phda/dataDetails</span></span></span>, Ancient Chinese Formula Database<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              https://www.ncmi.cn/phda/dataDetails</span></span></span>, the Catalogue of Ancient Classic Formulas published by the National Administration of Traditional Chinese Medicine<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              https://www.gov.cn/zhengce/zhengceku/2018-12/31/content_5429153.html</span></span></span>, and the National Medical Products Administration<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
              https://www.nmpa.gov.cn/xxgk/fgwj/gzwj/gzwjyp/20230531161831118.html</span></span></span>. The collected dataset includes disease names, formula components, preparation methods, and usage instructions from classical texts such as Huangdi Neijing, Bencao Gangmu, and Shanghan Zabing Lun (see Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ III-A Problem Definition ‣ III ZhiFangDanTai Model ‣ ZhiFangDanTai: Fine-tuning Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation Model for Traditional Chinese Medicine Formula" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> for examples). Since the dataset lacks fine-grained information such as the composition of the formula’s sovereign, minister, assistant, courier; efficacy; contraindications; tongue and pulse diagnosis, it cannot be directly used to fine-tune LLMs for generating TCM formulas and corresponding explanations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address the absence of fine-grained information in the original TCM dataset, we employ DeepSeek <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> to extract detailed information for each prescription using its name and ingredients as input.
Leveraging LLMs’ in-context learning capability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, we first annotate 50 samples as reference examples, then utilize the model to verify and refine the remaining data through redundant cleaning and formula interpretation. The resulting documents <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathit{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathit{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> contain seven key elements for each symptom <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>,
i.e., <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Disease</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recommended Formulas</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Herbal Ingredients</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Applicable Symptoms and Population</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pulse and Tongue Diagnosis</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Contraindications</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Preparation Methods</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>2 </span>Source Documents <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Text Chunks </h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We split <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathit{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathit{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> into several text chunks.
Selecting the chunk size is a trade-off issue, as shorter text chunks suffer from the high recall problem, while longer text chunks introduce redundant information and increases processing time for large models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Through empirical findings, we set the chunk size to 512 tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>3 </span>Text Chunks <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Entities &amp; Relations </h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prompt the LLM to extract instances of entities and the relationships
between entities from a given chunk, since recent studies by Wei <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> and Lopez et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> have demonstrated that LLMs outperform state-of-the-art deep learning models in entity extraction tasks. Based on this finding, we employ an LLM (e.g., DeepSeek-7B) for entity-relation extraction and subsequent relationship categorization. We define two types of relationships between entities: intra-category relationships occur when both entities belong to the same sub-community, while inter-category relationships exist when entities originate from different sub-communities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>4 </span>Entities &amp; Relations <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Knowledge Graph </h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Base on the extracted entities and relations, we construct knowledge graph <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> using neo4j (https://neo4j.com/), which enables quick location of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fine-grained information</span> and improves search accuracy during retrieval.
We define two types of relationships between entities: inter-category relationships occur when both entities belong to the same sub-community <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{C}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{C}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, while intra-category relationships exist when entities originate from different sub-communities
Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-B4 Entities &amp; Relations → Knowledge Graph ‣ III-B GraphRAG ‣ III ZhiFangDanTai Model ‣ ZhiFangDanTai: Fine-tuning Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation Model for Traditional Chinese Medicine Formula" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows a portion of the constructed knowledge graph <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas edges connecting nodes of the same color indicate intra-category relationships, while edges between differently colored nodes denote inter-category relationships.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.05867v1/KG.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:314/236;" width="314" height="236" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Constructed Knowledge Graph (Partial). We translate the knowledge graph (KG) into English for clarity.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>5 </span>Knowledge Graph <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Graph Communities <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Community Summaries </h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the knowledge graph <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, we identify diverse communities to analyze TCM formulas from multiple perspectives. We apply the Leiden community algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> in a hierarchical manner to detect sub-communities until no further splitting is possible.
The resulting communities are divided into seven categories, matching our <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fine-grained information</span> taxonomy, i.e., <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diseases</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recommended Formulas</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Herbal Ingredients</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Applicable Symptoms and Population</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pulse and Tongue Diagnosis</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Contraindications</span>, and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Preparation Methods</span>. Formally, we use <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{C_{1},\ldots,C_{7}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{C_{1},\ldots,C_{7}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to represent the detected communities.
Each community <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a set of entities <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{1},\ldots,e_{|C_{i}|}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{1},\ldots,e_{|C_{i}|}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a community description <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{|C_{i}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{|C_{i}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ III-B5 Knowledge Graph → Graph Communities → Community Summaries ‣ III-B GraphRAG ‣ III ZhiFangDanTai Model ‣ ZhiFangDanTai: Fine-tuning Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation Model for Traditional Chinese Medicine Formula" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> presents the basic information of the extracted fine-grained communities from the document.
After community division, we encode them into a vector library <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{C}=\{\vec{C_{1}},\ldots,\vec{C_{7}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{C}=\{\vec{C_{1}},\ldots,\vec{C_{7}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For implementation, we use LlamaIndex Library<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                
                
                
              https://docs.llamaindex.ai/en/stable/</span></span></span> for retrieval, with mxbai-embed-large<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
                
                
                
              https://inscode-doc.inscode.cc/</span></span></span> as the default encoder. Other encoders like OpenAI’s text-embedding-ada-002 can also be used.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE II</span>: </span><span id="S3.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fine-Grained Community Information.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T2.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T2.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:102.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T2.5.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Community Name</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:91.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T2.5.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Entity Examples</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:102.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T2.5.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Community Description</span></span>
</span></td>
